WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
If mowing stops, why does a sunny field not stay a field?

2
00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:06.700
Watch one Virginia Piedmont field.

3
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:14.140
The mower leaves. Seeds already in soil stir, while others arrive by wind or birds.

4
00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:21.287
Many populations live here together, forming a community.

5
00:00:22.707 --> 00:00:27.887
Ecological succession is change, over years to centuries, in which species live in

6
00:00:27.887 --> 00:00:32.967
an area. It often follows a general pattern, but not a fixed schedule.

7
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:37.310
Weeds and grasses grow quickly in the open field.

8
00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.450
Broomsedge and blackberry rise above shorter plants.

9
00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.170
Pine seedlings appear where seed trees are nearby.

10
00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:51.130
Their crowns climb into the bright space, and the sunny field begins changing its

11
00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:52.130
own future.

12
00:00:52.883 --> 00:00:55.343
The pines grow taller and their crowns join.

13
00:00:56.703 --> 00:00:58.983
A canopy now blocks much of the sunlight.

14
00:01:00.243 --> 00:01:02.243
Near the ground, the light meter falls.

15
00:01:03.623 --> 00:01:05.503
New pine seedlings grow poorly there.

16
00:01:06.803 --> 00:01:10.063
Some young hardwoods cope with shade better and survive below.

17
00:01:11.167 --> 00:01:13.967
Two seedlings now stand under the same canopy.

18
00:01:15.247 --> 00:01:17.287
One is pine and one is hardwood.

19
00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:20.507
Which is more likely to persist in the shade?

20
00:01:21.847 --> 00:01:22.847
Make your prediction.

21
00:01:28.833 --> 00:01:31.893
The pine seedling lags, while the hardwood survives.

22
00:01:33.273 --> 00:01:36.233
Later, an old pine dies and opens a bright gap.

23
00:01:37.413 --> 00:01:39.573
The hardwood grows faster into that space.

24
00:01:40.813 --> 00:01:42.273
No plant planned this change.

25
00:01:43.613 --> 00:01:46.973
Growth altered conditions, changing which plants survived well.

26
00:01:47.983 --> 00:01:52.723
Organisms change conditions, so suited species can become more common.

27
00:01:54.043 --> 00:01:55.663
Here, pine growth changed light.

28
00:01:56.903 --> 00:01:59.223
In other places, plants also change soil.

29
00:02:00.523 --> 00:02:05.023
The pattern comes from survival and growth, not from a goal or a finish line.

30
00:02:06.083 --> 00:02:08.203
Now compare two starting surfaces.

31
00:02:09.503 --> 00:02:13.503
An abandoned field still has soil, nutrients, seeds, and roots.

32
00:02:14.803 --> 00:02:16.623
This begins secondary succession.

33
00:02:18.043 --> 00:02:19.323
Bare rock has no soil.

34
00:02:20.943 --> 00:02:25.403
Primary succession begins there, with pioneer species that can start building soil.

35
00:02:26.467 --> 00:02:30.427
On bare rock, the pioneers are usually lichens and mosses.

36
00:02:31.687 --> 00:02:34.327
Glacial gravel can begin with other hardy plants.

37
00:02:35.807 --> 00:02:39.347
Soil develops slowly before many rooted plants can establish.

38
00:02:40.667 --> 00:02:45.647
Starting with soil is why secondary succession usually reaches forest faster.

39
00:02:46.717 --> 00:02:50.997
Back in the old field, pines may give way to hardwoods as light changes.

40
00:02:52.377 --> 00:02:57.197
In most of Virginia, the older model names oak-hickory forest as the late stage.

41
00:02:58.557 --> 00:03:03.257
Yet nearby seeds, soil, climate, and disturbance can change the route and timing.

42
00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:07.790
Follow the changing conditions, not only the plant names.

43
00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:12.030
That move explains why one stage can favor another.

44
00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:17.530
It also keeps a common path from becoming a promise about every field.
